        "slideId": "s9",
        "slideIndex": 8,
        "turn": 16,
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "The breakthrough lies in the 2D target embedding space: t-SNE replaces the Gaussian distribution with a Student-t distribution with one degree of freedom, also known as the Cauchy kernel. Because its power-law tail decays as inverse distance squared rather than exponentially, moderately separated clusters can spread out across the canvas without penalty.",
