  "ch11_p3_sim2_solo": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_sim2_solo.mp3",
    "duration": 18.072,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "Welcome to Interactive Lab 2: Multi-Strategy Convergence Studio! In Canvas 3, track empirical accuracy curves as BADGE hits ninety-eight percent, leaving pure margin sampling behind in a batch redundancy trap. Inspect diagnostic KPIs in Canvas 4!",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 18.072,
        "duration": 18.072
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_sim2_duo": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_sim2_duo.mp3",
    "duration": 13.848,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Kaito",
        "text": "Look at Canvas 3 in Interactive Lab 2! The cyan BADGE curve leaps to ninety-eight percent accuracy in only twenty-four samples, while the orange margin curve stalls at eighty-eight percent because it keeps re-sampling the same dense cluster!",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 13.848,
        "duration": 13.848
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_chk1_solo": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_chk1_solo.mp3",
    "duration": 29.376,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "Active Checkpoint 1: Consider a 3-class classifier with penultimate feature vector z equals [2.0, -1.0]. Boundary instance A has probabilities [0.49, 0.48, 0.03], and confident instance B has probabilities [0.98, 0.01, 0.01]. What are their gradient norms and why does k-means++ strongly favor instance A?",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 29.376,
        "duration": 29.376
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_chk1_duo": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_chk1_duo.mp3",
    "duration": 16.464,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Kaito",
        "text": "Checkpoint 1 time! Let's compute BADGE hallucinated gradients: instance A is heavily contested on the boundary between classes 1 and 2, while instance B is ninety-eight percent confident. How do their gradient norms and squared sampling probabilities compare?",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 16.464,
        "duration": 16.464
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_chk1_feedback_correct": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_chk1_feedback_correct.mp3",
    "duration": 21.888,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "Outstanding derivation! Option B is correct. The gradient norm for instance A is one point five six seven five, while for confident instance B it is only zero point zero five four eight. This twenty-eight point six fold norm ratio results in an eight hundred eighteen fold probability advantage under k-means++ sampling!",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 21.888,
        "duration": 21.888
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_chk1_feedback_incorrect": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_chk1_feedback_incorrect.mp3",
    "duration": 22.44,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "Not quite. Option B is correct. Remember that the gradient block is the residual probability times the feature embedding. Boundary instance A produces large residuals of negative zero point five one and positive zero point four eight, producing a norm of one point five six seven five, over twenty-eight times larger than confident instance B.",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 22.44,
        "duration": 22.44
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_chk2_solo": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_chk2_solo.mp3",
    "duration": 12.48,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "Active Checkpoint 2: In monotone submodular facility location optimization, why is the greedy algorithm mathematically guaranteed to achieve at least sixty-three point two percent of the optimal coverage?",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 12.48,
        "duration": 12.48
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_chk2_duo": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_chk2_duo.mp3",
    "duration": 14.712,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Kaito",
        "text": "Checkpoint 2! Recall Nemhauser's celebrated theorem on submodular diminishing returns: each greedy choice shrinks the remaining suboptimality gap by 1 minus 1 over b. What is the compounding limit over b steps?",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 14.712,
        "duration": 14.712
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_chk2_feedback_correct": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_chk2_feedback_correct.mp3",
    "duration": 18.672,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "Brilliant mathematical proof! Option A is correct. Applying the recurrence relation over b steps bounds the remaining gap by 1 minus 1 over b raised to the power b, which is strictly less than 1 over e. Subtracting yields the certified sixty-three point two percent approximation bound!",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 18.672,
        "duration": 18.672
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_chk2_feedback_incorrect": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_chk2_feedback_incorrect.mp3",
    "duration": 18.504,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "That's not right. Option A is the correct answer. The diminishing returns property guarantees that at each step, the greedy choice captures at least 1 over b of the remaining distance to OPT. Compounding over b steps yields 1 minus 1 over e, or sixty-three point two percent.",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 18.504,
        "duration": 18.504
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_chk3_solo": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_chk3_solo.mp3",
    "duration": 13.488,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "Active Checkpoint 3: In an industrial batch perception pipeline with batch size b equals 500, which architectural design prevents the model from querying redundant video frames or falling into chemical analog traps?",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 13.488,
        "duration": 13.488
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_chk3_duo": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_chk3_duo.mp3",
    "duration": 14.784,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Kaito",
        "text": "Final checkpoint! As Novartis and Cruise proved, querying high uncertainty points independently causes batch redundancy collapse. What strategy combination guarantees both boundary uncertainty and geometric diversity?",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 14.784,
        "duration": 14.784
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_chk3_feedback_correct": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_chk3_feedback_correct.mp3",
    "duration": 18.432,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "Spot on! Option B is correct. BADGE hallucinated gradient embeddings combined with k-means++ seeding simultaneously maximize gradient norm for boundary uncertainty and enforce orthogonal spatial dispersion, eliminating batch redundancy and breaking analog traps!",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 18.432,
        "duration": 18.432
      }
    ]
  },
  "ch11_p3_chk3_feedback_incorrect": {
    "audioFile": "ch11_p3_chk3_feedback_incorrect.mp3",
    "duration": 18.504,
    "sentences": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "Incorrect. Option B is correct. Relying on margin or entropy alone causes batch collapse onto redundant clusters. Combining hallucinated gradients with k-means++ diverse seeding enforces geometric orthogonality, solving the batch redundancy pathology.",
        "start": 0,
        "end": 18.504,
        "duration": 18.504
      }
    ]
  },
