        "slideId": "s3",
        "slideIndex": 2,
        "turn": 5,
        "speaker": "Dr. Nova",
        "text": "By Donsker's Invariance Principle, normalized random walks converge weakly to independent Wiener processes W1 and W2. Because cumulative paths linger away from zero, sample covariance scales as order T rather than T to the minus one-half. Consequently, Pearson r does NOT converge to zero—its limiting distribution piles up near plus or minus one!",
